---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import Topo from '../components/contour/Topo.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site';
---

<Base
  title="Résumé"
  description="Résumé of James Burch: senior software engineer and tech lead in fintech, building GitHub Copilot agents for engineering teams."
>
  <Topo
    x="85%"
    y="80px"
    fade="linear-gradient(to bottom, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.8) 0, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.8) 320px, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35) 560px, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35) 100%)"
  />
  <div class="head-band">
  <header class="container page-head head on-map">
    <h1>Résumé</h1>
    <p class="lede">AI agent engineering and technical leadership in fintech.</p>
    <Button href={SITE.resumePdf} download="James-Burch-Resume.pdf" arrow>Download PDF</Button>
  </header>
  </div>

  <div class="container">
    <article class="resume">
      <section aria-labelledby="r-summary">
        <h2 id="r-summary" class="label">Summary</h2>
        <p>
          Senior software engineer and tech lead who designs AI agent tooling for engineering teams. Designed and built a
          GitHub Copilot agent system (custom agents, skills, guardrail instructions and scripts) that the team uses for
          code review, delivery and production triage, with scripted checks on agent output and approval gates. Tech lead on Charles Schwab’s advisor money-movement platform, with nearly
          six years shipping regulated fintech APIs in C#/.NET and Angular.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="r-exp">
        <h2 id="r-exp" class="label">Experience</h2>
        <div class="job">
          <h3>Charles Schwab</h3>
          <p class="role"><strong>Senior Software Engineer, Tech Lead</strong> · Jan 2021 – present</p>
          <p class="meta">Lone Tree, Colorado</p>
          <p class="context">
            Advisor money-movement platform: the APIs and tools financial advisors and outside firms use to move client
            cash and securities.
          </p>

          <h4>AI-driven engineering</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Built a GitHub Copilot agent system for a multi-repo .NET and Angular codebase, in use on my team and
              shared with the wider engineering group.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Verified output:</strong> scripts gather the facts, the model writes its findings as structured
              data, and a validator rejects any file, line, commit or number that is not in those facts.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Review:</strong> a code-review agent checks C# and Angular changes against team checklists; a
              team-review agent prepares review notes for every open pull request.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Delivery:</strong> a story-delivery agent takes a story or bug end to end across repos, from the
              ticket to code, tests, pull request and QA handoff.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Production triage:</strong> an agent turns a single correlation ID into a log trace and root cause
              across services; a regression skill finds the change that introduced a defect.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Context engineering:</strong> per-service context docs, durable repo memory and onboarding skills
              give agents, and new engineers, an accurate map of each system.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Guardrails:</strong> dry run by default; explicit approval for each irreversible or outbound
              action, tied to the reviewed commit; isolated clones for automation; OWASP Top 10 rules that treat model
              output as untrusted input.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Everyday workflow:</strong> scripts for cross-repo standup digests, pre-push checks and flaky-test
              detection, a .NET local integration harness that needs no repository edits, and an audit of the agent
              setup against real session history.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Platform and leadership</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Tech lead (acting in 2025, formally since Nov 2025): lead delivery of third-party APIs for several types
              of money movement, built for partner firms.
            </li>
            <li>
              Set up performance engineering with log-based endpoint monitoring and before/after automated tests; own
              API contract governance (OpenAPI, service registry, API gateway tokens).
            </li>
            <li>
              Modernized a core application from SOAP to REST and built a new transfer capability for advisors, both
              with team recognition awards; shipped firm-configurable approval rules that retired a legacy interface.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="r-skills">
        <h2 id="r-skills" class="label">Skills</h2>
        <dl class="skills">
          <div>
            <dt>AI engineering</dt>
            <dd>Agent design; GitHub Copilot custom agents, skills and instructions; context and prompt engineering; LLM output validation; human-in-the-loop approvals; agent guardrails; MCP</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Languages and frameworks</dt>
            <dd>C#, .NET, ASP.NET Core, Entity Framework, TypeScript, Angular, Python, PowerShell, SQL</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Platform</dt>
            <dd>REST and OpenAPI, microservices, Apache Kafka, SQL Server, MongoDB, Splunk, API gateway, GitHub, CI/CD, xUnit</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="r-edu">
        <h2 id="r-edu" class="label">Education and recognition</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>B.S. Computer Science</strong>, Colorado Technical University, 2019</li>
          <li><strong>Microsoft Technology Associate:</strong> Software Development Fundamentals, 2021</li>
          <li><strong>GitHub Trusted Committer,</strong> Charles Schwab, 2025</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Schwab recognition awards (2023–2026),</strong> including team awards for two production launches and
            an award for third-party API work
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head-band {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-3);
  }
  .head .lede {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-6);
  }
  .resume {
    max-width: 56rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .resume {
      padding: clamp(32px, 4vw, 56px);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--r-lg);
      background: var(--surface);
    }
  }
  section + section {
    margin-top: var(--s-7);
  }
  h2.label {
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-4);
    padding-bottom: var(--s-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  h3 {
    font-size: var(--fs-h3);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-1);
  }
  .role {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .meta {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-3);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .context {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  h4 {
    margin: var(--s-6) 0 var(--s-3);
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }
  li::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .skills {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .skills div {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-1);
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
  }
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .skills div {
      grid-template-columns: 14rem 1fr;
      gap: var(--s-4);
    }
  }
  .skills dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .skills dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
